WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:03.800
 You should probably read
all your poems out loud.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.050
Dogs are very good
to read poetry to.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:07.250
Cats, not so much.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:08.210
Fish are good.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:11.480
If you have a goldfish, your
first audience before you

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.160
send your poetry
out into the world.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.050
Poetry does not need a story.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.540
That's not what its function is.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.130
Poetry makes a lot
of people tense.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.530
You speak English, this
poem is in English,

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:26.830
and yet you have no
idea what it means.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:31.690
A lot of poetry begins as a
secret and covert activity.

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:36.070
So poetry is sort of a diary
without the lock, a diary

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.210
that you want people to read.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.380
A poem often has two subjects,
the starting subject and then

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:44.670
the discovered subject.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.890
"I was as sure as a boy could
be that this useless, worthless

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.930
thing I wove out
of boredom would

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.150
be enough to make us even."

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.400
The poem wants to
leave the object

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.350
and go beyond it into
something greater.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.320
This is what poets are paid for,
I mean, to like look at clouds,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.239
watch chipmunks.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.310
Someone has to keep an
eye on these things.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.540
I like to make a real mess.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.270
I like to doodle,
cross things out.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.132
I try to write a
good line, and then

00:01:15.132 --> 00:01:16.840
another good line,
and another good line.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.890
But no one's a
good line machine,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.450
so there's a lot of
staring involved.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:26.110
Poetry provides us with a
history of the human heart.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.590
If you trace it back to the
oldest poems we can find,

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.090
the emotions are the same.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.000
Underneath it all, we're
all after the same thing.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.970
When you read something
back that it just

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:38.980
has your mark on it,
no one else could

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.750
have written that but you.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.530
This voice is just
yours, and yours alone.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.560
I'm Billy Collins, and
this is my MasterClass.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:54.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

